\title{Warp}

\p{A \em{warp} is the unit of scheduling on an NVIDIA GPU: a group of ~32 threads that issue one instruction per cycle in lockstep. Each functional unit in the [SM](OGCS6) has as many lanes as a warp has threads, so a single fetched instruction fans out across all 32 lanes at once. The warp — not the thread — is what the hardware fetches, schedules, and tracks; the per-thread [[7F19]] programming model is an illusion built on top of it.}

\p{Because the warp is the atom of execution, the two facts that dominate GPU performance are both properties of the warp: it [hides memory latency by switching among resident warps](1PWL) instead of stalling, and it [serializes both sides of a branch](OGCSM) when its threads diverge.}
